clang's builtin stdatomic.h (and other clang resource-dir headers) wrap C++ interop blocks in:
  #ifdef __cplusplus
  extern "C" {
  #endif
  ...
  #ifdef __cplusplus
  }
  #endif

Since #ifdef/#ifndef conditions are kept symbolic (Condition::Defined) for later per-config resolution (the polyvariant design), the parser must be able to grammatically parse BOTH branches' text as valid C during the initial polyvariant-AST build, even though __cplusplus is never actually defined for a C compile. That means 'extern "C" { ... }' has to parse as a (no-op, in C) linkage-specification block, but currently the declaration parser hits it as if starting a normal declaration and fails with 'expected declaration type, found StringLit("C")' at the 'extern "C" {' line.

